WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.560
- Today in this video,

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.510
I will share with you what the jobs are

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.780
that in 2030 will be in very high demand.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.420
And also what the jobs
are that in the future

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.240
likely will not be
needed that much anymore.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.820
And all of this is not just
based on my gut feeling,

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.260
but it's based on a study

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.600
from Boston Consulting Group called

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.040
the Future of Jobs in the Era of AI.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.060
So, let's look into it.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.240
Let's first better
understand their methodology.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.130
Indeed, they're looking at two factors,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.460
workforce demand and workforce supply.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.050
Let's start with the latter.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.570
So, for the workforce supply,
they're looking at graduates

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.210
from education system, from university.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.510
They're looking at migration
and at retirement rate.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.950
And for the workforce demand,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.930
they're looking at technology advancement,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.450
advancements in artificial intelligence.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.129
They look at efficiency
increases that can be expected

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:52.890
in the different industries.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.830
And they also look at the effect of COVID,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.380
and they specifically
are modeling the effects

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.110
of all these factors,
workforce supply and demand

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.090
until 2030.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.940
And the first main, maybe
counterintuitive, takeaway

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.600
is that, contrary to popular
demand, actually, in 2030,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.780
it's not the case that
AI eats up all the jobs,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.020
but actually, there will be
a shortfall of the workforce.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.450
So there are not enough people
that actually are needed

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.780
for the jobs net overall,
the different job categories.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.100
This is 6.7 million people
that are missing in the US

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.670
and 1.3 million people that
are missing in Germany.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:30.960
So let's start with the US.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.730
What are the job families with
the highest demand in 2030

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.530
according to Boston Consulting Group?

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.840
And I will first talk about job families,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.370
and later in this video, at the end,

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:44.203
also share some very,
very specific positions

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:47.190
in these families that will be missing.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.260
Let's start with number five.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.770
And these are roles in
architecture and engineering.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.400
Number four is business
and financial operations.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.010
So, professionals working
in business roles,

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.710
and specifically in finance roles.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.560
I trust this is good news
for all of us consultants,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.560
who surely usually are
qualitatively inclined,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.870
have a certain analytical understanding,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.080
which definitely will be helpful
for these types of careers.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.350
Next is healthcare practitioners
and technical support.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.510
I also believe that this is no surprise.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.420
All of these things will
not just be replaced by AI

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.730
and, surely, with our aging society,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.010
the need for healthcare will increase.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.220
Next is management,
white collar management

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.600
leadership roles.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.760
This I found to be
actually quite interesting,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.310
because this is an area where I often hear

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.830
that AI will replace all these positions.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.780
Apparently, it's not so much the case,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.640
at least not until 2030.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.300
Last, but not least, by far
the most significant demand,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.550
even twice the magnitude of, for instance,

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.090
management, is computer and mathematics.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.250
So, likely, these are
the software engineers,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.220
the professionals potentially
developing all these systems

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.810
of the future, the AI, that then,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.600
maybe or maybe not might
replace any of these jobs.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.790
Surely, these people will be needed.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.820
There's a career that will
require quite formal education.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.010
So, if you are willing
to invest in yourself

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.710
and inclined for these
types of topics, go ahead.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.990
But now let's look at the
other side of the matter.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.440
What are the positions we
actually will have too many people

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.830
working with these types of
qualifications in the future,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.700
and here on number three,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.460
it's transportation and material moving.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.960
So, sure they can very much understand

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.690
why these things might be
automated in the future.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.120
Many trains might drive
without train drivers.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.400
Cars will drive without drivers.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.460
So, many people operating
these types of vehicles

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.100
will likely not be needed
anymore in the future.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.270
Number two of the positions

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.640
with the most significant
workforce surplus

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.086
is actually food preparation and service.

00:03:44.086 --> 00:03:46.800
So, apparently, these types
of roles are expected to be

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.720
much more automated as well, potential,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.670
so the demand for them declining.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.640
Trust that makes sense also
in the light of food delivery,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.730
quick food delivery
services, and on number one,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.630
the role that in the US will
be most likely not needed,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.330
at least not to the extent
as right now in the future

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.360
is office and administrative support.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.770
So, I think you have the takeaways

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.440
that these are the more
simple business roles,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.810
the support type of business roles

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.430
that maybe don't really require

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.620
this high level of qualification

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.230
as, for instance, the
management leadership roles,

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.850
also the business operations,

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.070
finance roles we just talked about.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.080
So, yes, if you work in business

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.910
and your qualifications are not that high,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.030
you're not really specialized,
not really experienced,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.680
this might be a problem.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.860
This is a job family that
will be at risk in the future.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.080
And let's now switch to
Germany, and surprising,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.470
or maybe not surprising,
the jobs that will be

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.047
in high demand are actually
quite similar than in the US.

00:04:43.047 --> 00:04:45.780
The numbers of course, a
bit different, a bit smaller

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.090
due to the lower population in Germany.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.860
There's one notice of
an exception, though,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.950
and this is that people working
in educational instruction

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.930
and library will be needed.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.710
So, education professionals
are in quite demand in Germany.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.810
They seem to be missing in the future.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.520
If we look at the job families
where there will actually

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.160
be a workforce surplus in the future,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.960
I think it's pretty
interesting that number three,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.390
we find construction and extraction.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.330
The common narrative,
at least in Germany is

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.850
that actually there is a
big lag of qualified people

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.440
working in these sectors.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.480
Apparently, here, the experts
of Boston Consulting Group

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.850
are predicting that this
will change in the future.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.550
Office and administrative
support we just talked about.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.940
Interestingly enough, for
number one is production,

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.100
where they're expected to
be more people available

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.050
in the workforce that are actually needed.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.360
Also this, I think quite
interesting, in Germany

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.950
there's a lot of talk about these people

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.870
actually being in demand and missing,

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.110
about a need for these
qualified blue collar workers.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.940
Apparently, at least in the future,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.860
this might not be the case anymore,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.170
largely, of course, likely
also due to automation.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.030
Now, the study from
Boston Consulting Group

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.980
even went one step further

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.510
because now they're double clicked to into

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.990
these job families to really
discern the tangible positions

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.200
that likely will be in demand
or not that much in demand

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.160
in the future.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.830
Now, let me share the top five positions

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.720
for the US and for Germany.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.670
On number five, it's engineers.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.230
I don't think it's that surprising,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.240
given the other figures that we just saw.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.460
On number four, it's medical doctors.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.280
I think these also very much
in line with what we just saw.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.730
On number three, business
operations specialists,

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.500
and on number two, and this is, I think,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.740
what was quite interesting,
senior executives,

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.250
really senior leaders
of large organizations.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.970
This is what will be in
high demand in the US,

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.390
only followed by on number
one, computer occupations.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.040
And if you look at the
figures for Germany,

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.630
it's more or less the same story,

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.380
only with teachers stepping
in, actually, on number three.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.700
So, what does this mean for us?

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.990
What does this mean for you?

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.000
Given that I know that at
least a good portion of you

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.730
have a professional
background in consulting,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:48.930
I think roles like these,

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.140
so, senior executives and
business operations specialists,

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.050
are roles that you will
be very well equipped

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.030
after you've spent a
couple of years working

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.430
for one of the leading
consulting firms like McKinsey,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.260
BCG, Bain, and the likes.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.420
So, I think this is
actually quite good news,

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:04.590
but now, you let me know.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.660
What is your perception on this topic?

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.280
Let me know in the comments
about your opinion.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.080
I would very much appreciate your view.

